1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,280
Hey, everyone.  We're going to be taking a look at why employee interest might not align with firm interest.

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:08,640
Imagine this.  You run a chain of phone stores.

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:12,160
Your sales team earns $18 an hour, no matter what.

4
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:16,160
Some are trying, others, well, they're just getting through the day.

5
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:17,760
Sales are slipping.

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,960
Customers are also slipping through sales as well.

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:26,760
You start asking yourself, why aren't my employees trying harder?  It's not laziness.

8
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:27,960
It's a broken system.

9
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:29,840
They don't benefit when the company does.

10
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:31,080
And that's the real problem.

11
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:37,480
This situation is a textbook example of something called the principal agent problem.

12
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:44,760
When the person doing the work, that is the agent, doesn't share the same goals as the person paying for it, that is the principal.

13
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:48,200
This leads to what we call incentive conflict here.

14
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:52,720
What's best for the employee isn't necessarily what's best for the business.

15
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:58,080
And to manage that conflict, businesses often face agency costs.

16
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:04,880
These are costs of monitoring employees, offering bonuses, or fixing problems caused by misaligned incentives.

17
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,680
And lastly, there's asymmetric information.

18
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:10,880
When one party knows more than the other.

19
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:13,040
And that causes two major problems.

20
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,320
Adverse selection.

21
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:19,800
You hire someone who looks great on paper but coast once they're in, and moral hazard.

22
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:25,520
Once they're hired, they know they can slack off because their pay doesn't depend on performance.

23
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,320
Let's get back to the phone store.

24
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:30,240
Your team has no skin in the game.

25
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:33,840
Whether they sell five phones or none, the paycheck is the same.

26
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:36,440
This is where moral hazard creeps in.

27
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:42,960
When employees don't bear the consequences of poor performance, they also don't feel pressured to do more than the minimum.

28
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:47,640
There's no downside for slacking off, and there's no upside for going above and beyond.

29
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:51,560
The system quietly signals, don't bother, it won't matter.

30
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:59,000
And if you hired someone who looks sharp at the interview but under delivers on the floor, then that's adverse selection in action.

31
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:04,760
You attracted someone who was good at getting the job, not necessarily at doing the job.

32
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:10,760
Both of these problems, moral hazard and adverse selection, stem from misaligned incentives.

33
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:14,160
Employees have different goals than you do as the owner.

34
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,440
And without the right structure in place, those gaps grow wider.

35
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:23,120
So we need to identify the problem and properly align incentives.

36
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:28,080
We know that there may be suffering from some form of adverse selection or moral hazard.

37
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:29,360
So let's fix the problem.

38
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:32,640
Let's first start by aligning incentives.

39
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:37,520
Suppose our employees earn $18 an hour and work eight hours a day.

40
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:40,640
That's $144 for one day's work.

41
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:45,200
The issue is this may not align with the company's interest in selling phones.

42
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:49,520
Now let's add a commission of $50 a phone.

43
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:53,680
The same employee now has more motivation to sell phones.

44
00:02:54,800 --> 00:03:00,880
With eight hours worked and five phones sold, the employee now earns $394 for the day.

45
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:03,680
Now they win when the company wins.

46
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:08,160
Next, we might have to look at setting measurable goals.

47
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:10,880
Not every sale tells the full story.

48
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:14,560
Sometimes the best employees aren't the ones closing the most.

49
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:19,680
They're the ones offering great service, upselling with care and earning glowing reviews.

50
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:27,440
So instead of just rewarding raw sales numbers, build a system that values customer satisfaction.

51
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:33,800
Upsell success, where the agent sells a more expensive product or adds additional items to the product purchase.

52
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:39,800
Follow up diligence, where the agent stays in contact with potential or recent customers.

53
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:41,960
And then consistent professionalism.

54
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:47,880
Now, when your goals reflect the full customer experience, you send a message.

55
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:50,920
We value how you sell, not just what you sell.

56
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:53,640
Let's move on to the last step.

57
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:59,400
We've set the incentives, but we also want to make sure that our employees are following through.

58
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:02,760
Here we're looking at transparency and monitoring.

59
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,640
You can't improve what you don't measure.

60
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:09,240
So let's make performance visible to both leadership and staff.

61
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,960
We're going to use some tools like mystery shoppers.

62
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:13,720
Conversion rate tracking.

63
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:18,920
This is where you measure the number of people who buy an item over how many people who actually walk into the store.

64
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:21,720
And then peer reviews or team based metrics.

65
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:31,120
When employees know they're being evaluated fairly and consistently, they're more likely to stay engaged, focused and aligned with the company goals.

66
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:35,360
Taken together, these steps don't just reduce agency cost.

67
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:39,040
They've replaced control with clarity and discipline with direction.

68
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:44,400
But we're not done.  We've aligned incentives and put in other metrics.

69
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:45,440
But there's a catch.

70
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:54,920
When you introduce performance pay such as commissions and bonuses, you're not just motivating employees, you, you're also shifting risk to them.

71
00:04:55,640 --> 00:05:01,560
We have a good economy where we can sell a lot of phones and a bad economy where we don't sell as many phones.

72
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:05,800
In the good economy, we sell five phones and earn a commission of $250.

73
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:12,040
In the bad economy, we only sell two phones and earn a commission of $100.

74
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:16,000
This creates a hit to the employee's pay risk.

75
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,240
In this case, is the economy being good or bad playing a big role in compensation?

76
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:27,000
Even if they give it their all, their income now depends on things outside of their control.

77
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:29,880
The economy, foot traffic, competition.

78
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:31,720
The risk isn't free.

79
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:36,040
Here we know that more risk will require higher compensation.

80
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:41,400
So most solutions to moral hazard and adverse selection come with these trade offs.

81
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:49,800
We adopt incentive compensation only if its benefits, like employees working harder or smarter, outweigh its costs, like having to pay more to offset the risk.

82
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:55,240
We want to motivate better behavior and create extra value that outweighs the cost.

83
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:57,960
And we can measure that against two things.

84
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:01,840
First, status quo or flat pay.

85
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:07,000
Here we ask is this better than just keeping everyone at $18 an hour with no bonus?

86
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:15,080
And then the second measurable item, other possible solutions where we ask could we get the same improvement with less cost?

87
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:21,440
Maybe through better training, better hiring, team based rewards or non cash recognition.

88
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:26,120
But we don't have to choose between motivation and fairness.

89
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:30,240
There are ways to design smarter systems that manage the risk.

90
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:37,600
Here we can look at base and bonus pay, keep a steady paycheck, then reward strong performance on top.

91
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:41,600
This protects income while still driving effort.

92
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:48,160
We can offer team incentives, group rewards, reduce individual income swings and boost collaboration.

93
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:52,000
Look at profit sharing, let employees win with the company.

94
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:55,360
Long term alignment equals long term motivation.

95
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:03,680
And then lastly, look at minimum guarantees, set commission floor so even on slow days, income doesn't fall off a cliff.

96
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:08,880
Now, when we manage our risk smartly, incentive pay can do what it's supposed to do.

97
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:18,280
It can align behavior, boost performance and build loyalty without pushing our team over the edge of so here's the big idea.

98
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:21,240
People respond to the system you build.

99
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:25,080
It's not about good employees versus bad employees.

100
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:29,480
It's about what your structure rewards and what it ignores.

101
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:34,280
On the left we see what happens under bad incentives.  Flat pay.

102
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:36,440
No feedback, no recognitions.

103
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:45,120
Employees are disengaged, the store feels lifeless, customers are walking out and no one seems to care because the system doesn't ask them to.

104
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:48,800
On the right side, things look a little bit different.  There's energy.

105
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:51,280
Salespeople are present, helpful and motivated.

106
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:54,400
They're not just showing up, they're showing up to win.

107
00:07:54,720 --> 00:07:59,040
And that's what happens when incentives and expectations are aligned.

108
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:08,240
Let's wrap this up with incentives that work, match rewards, reward what matters, not just what's easy to track.

109
00:08:09,080 --> 00:08:10,440
Compensate fairly for risk.

110
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:14,520
If you're asking employees to carry uncertainty, pay them for it.

111
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:17,240
And then lastly, design with human behavior in mind.

112
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:20,520
Systems should work with people, not against them.

113
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:22,840
Incentives aren't just about money.

114
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:26,440
They're about motivation, trust and alignment.

115
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:28,080
Set the right structure.

116
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:31,400
One that's going to encourage ownership, effort and care.

117
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:35,160
Not because you're watching, but because people feel invested.

118
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:36,880
Set the right incentives.

119
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:41,040
Trust your people and support them with a system that works.

120
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:42,480
And that's all that we have.

121
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:43,600
Hope you've enjoyed it.